@wp-rocket @plugin
Feature: WP Rocket's page cache, from activation to removal

  The `@plugin` hooks install the build under test, licensed by a dummy key, and settle it with one
  logged-in admin request, which writes the config file its cache reads. The visitor here is not
  logged in: WP Rocket caches no logged-in user by default. A page served from WP Rocket's cache is
  told apart from a page WordPress generated by WP Rocket's own footprint — the cache file's copy
  carries a `cached@` timestamp, and a copy dated before this install is an earlier run's —, never by
  timing. Caching needs `WP_CACHE`, which WP Rocket writes into `wp-config.php` as the site's commands
  (activation) or as the web server (an admin request): a site whose `wp-config.php` neither can write
  skips the scenario.

  Removing WP Rocket leaves what the public sees, and what its uninstall owns, as before; the files
  it edits in place (`wp-config.php`'s `WP_CACHE`, its `.htaccess` block, `advanced-cache.php`) are
  then put back by the `@plugin` teardown, byte for byte, which warns about anything it could not.

  Scenario: WP Rocket serves a cached page once activated, and once removed its page footprint and the folders its uninstall deletes are gone
    Given WP Rocket has turned on WordPress's page cache
    When an anonymous visitor opens the home page twice
    Then WP Rocket handled the page of the first visit
    And the page of the second visit is served from WP Rocket's cache
    When WP Rocket is removed from the site
    Then an anonymous visitor's home page carries no trace of WP Rocket
    And nothing WP Rocket's uninstall deletes is left on the site
